"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][18]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M18-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M18-L1 – Bayesian Decision Theory and Loss<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Master: Probabilistic Models and Bayesian Inference (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate posterior risks and\ndistinguish decisions from estimates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Probabilities are not decisions<\/h2>\n<p>After a measurement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan automated inspection system estimates that a component is defective\nwith probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe workshop can ship it or reinspect it. Which action makes sense\ndepends not only on the probability but also on the consequences. We\ndescribe these consequences by a loss function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\theta,a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the unknown state and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nan admissible action. In the Bayesian model, uncertainty after\nobservation is described by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(\\theta\\mid x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor continuous states, an integral replaces the sum.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/munder><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(a\\mid x)=E[L(\\theta,a)\\mid x],\\qquad a^*(x)\\in\\arg\\min_a\\rho(a\\mid x).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The Bayes risk of a decision rule across all possible observations,\nby contrast, is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_X[\\rho(a(X)\\mid X)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt serves to compare entire rules within the assumed joint model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision with asymmetric costs<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001521_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zwei Risikogeraden schneiden sich bei Defektwahrscheinlichkeit 0,20; ab dort ist Nachprüfen günstiger.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zwei Risikogeraden schneiden sich bei\nDefektwahrscheinlichkeit 0,20; ab dort ist Nachprüfen\ngünstiger.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Entscheiden nach erwartetem Verlust.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The following amounts in euros are freely chosen teaching values.\nReinspection always costs 12; shipping costs 0 for an intact component\nand 60 for a defective one. This gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Reinspect<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(\\text{Reinspect})=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Ship<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(\\text{Ship})=60p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two risk lines intersect at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshipping costs 15 euros in expectation; reinspection has the lower risk.\nAt equality both actions are optimal. A probability of 25% is therefore\nnot the same as a recommendation to ship as a matter of principle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Point estimation under different losses<\/h2>\n<p>For real-valued parameters under squared loss, the decomposition<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[(\\theta-a)^2\\mid x]=\\operatorname{Var}(\\theta\\mid x)+(E[\\theta\\mid x]-a)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>yields the posterior mean as the optimal estimate, provided the\nsecond moment is finite. Under absolute loss, a posterior median\nminimises the risk. Under discrete 0–1 loss, an action with the greatest\nposterior mass is optimal; the mode of a continuous density does not\nfollow directly from the same argument because individual points then\nhave probability zero. The mode can also depend on the\nparametrisation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>states<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> prob<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(states<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>prob,<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(a,prob<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>((states<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),prob<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>abs(states<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>([<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Reinspection now costs 18 euros and a missed defect costs 90 euros.\nAt what probability should reinspection take place? Which point estimate\ndoes absolute loss give in the discrete code example?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: threshold\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>18<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>90<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">18/90=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nreinspect at higher values. The median is 1, because the cumulative mass\nreaches 0.8 there, whereas at 0 it is only 0.4. Changes in costs, model\ncalibration and uncertainty about the costs themselves can alter the\ndecision. A loss matrix is therefore an explicit domain assumption, not\na priority that can automatically be inferred from the data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://faculty.washington.edu/yenchic/26A_stat581/Lec1_decision.html\">University\nof Washington, STAT 581: Elementary Decision Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Posterior\nrisk, beta-binomial<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M18-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M18-L2 – Conjugate Models and Hierarchical Bayesian Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Master: Probabilistic Models and Bayesian Inference (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L6<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive conjugate updates and\nmodel group information using partial pooling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conjugacy as a computational aid<\/h2>\n<p>In a workshop, independent yes/no inspections are recorded for each\nproduction line. The success probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis assigned the prior distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Beta<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Beta}(a,b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith density proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta^{a-1}(1-\\theta)^{b-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\theta&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuccesses from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninspections, the likelihood has parameter-dependent part\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta^k(1-\\theta)^{n-k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMultiplying the two functions adds the exponents; the subsequently\nnormalised density is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Beta<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\mid k,n\\sim\\operatorname{Beta}(a+k,b+n-k).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The distribution family is preserved, so the prior is called\nconjugate. This simplifies calculations but does not prove that the\nprior is substantively appropriate. The posterior mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a+k)/(a+b+n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe probability of success at the next conditionally independent\ninspection is exactly this mean.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Groups with different sample sizes<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001526_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Rohe Quoten und Posterior-Mittel bei Stichprobengrößen 2, 20 und 80; kleine Gruppen schrumpfen stärker.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Rohe Quoten und Posterior-Mittel bei\nStichprobengrößen 2, 20 und 80; kleine Gruppen schrumpfen\nstärker.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Schrumpfung durch gemeinsame Prior.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>With a common Beta(4,4) prior, three lines yield the following\nsynthetic results:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Line<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Successes<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Inspections<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Raw rate<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Posterior mean<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">15<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.75<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">19/28<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">60<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">80<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.75<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">64/88<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The graphic compares both measures; the small group is pulled more\nstrongly towards the prior centre of 0.5. With complete pooling, all\nlines would share the same parameter. With separate models and no common\ninformation base, estimates would not depend on other groups. Fixed\ncommon hyperparameters as used here provide regularisation, but no\ninformation is yet learned from the other groups of observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hierarchical extension<\/h2>\n<p>A genuinely hierarchical model assumes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Beta<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_j\\mid m,\\kappa\\sim\\operatorname{Beta}(m\\kappa,(1-m)\\kappa)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand assigns hyperpriors to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\in(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the common centre and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe concentration. Each group contributes information to the posterior\nof the hyperparameters. Conditional on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m,\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe beta update remains analytical; their full uncertainty can be\nintegrated numerically, for example. In empirical Bayes, by contrast,\nhyperparameters are estimated from the data and then treated as fixed.\nThis can underestimate hyperparameter uncertainty.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.stats <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> beta<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>k<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a,b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>k,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>k<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(a<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(a<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b),[<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">19<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">28<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">64<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">88<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(beta.ppf(<span class=\"fl\">.025<\/span>,a,b),beta.ppf(<span class=\"fl\">.975<\/span>,a,b))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>With Beta(4,4), four successes are observed out of five. Calculate\nthe posterior and the prediction of another success. What happens for a\ngroup without data?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: Beta(8,5), prediction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>13<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8/13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout data, the prior remains unchanged when the hyperparameters are\nfixed. In a hierarchical model, a new group can nevertheless learn from\nother groups through the posterior distribution of the hyperparameters,\nprovided the exchangeability assumption is defensible in the domain.\nDifferent types of machines may require separate group structures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://faculty.washington.edu/yenchic/26A_stat581/Lec1_decision.html\">University\nof Washington, STAT 581: Elementary Decision Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Posterior\nrisk, beta-binomial<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mc-stan.org/learn-stan/case-studies/pool-binary-trials.html\">Stan\nCase Study: Hierarchical Partial Pooling for Repeated Binary Trials<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Hierarchical models, partial pooling<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M18-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M18-L3 – Graphical Models and Conditional Independence<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Master: Probabilistic Models and Bayesian Inference (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, M13, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Factorise directed graphical\nmodels and explain d-separation using a collider.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local distributions instead of enormous tables<\/h2>\n<p>A plant has a cooling state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na temperature state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a warning signal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(the original model identifiers are retained). One possible directed\nacyclic graph is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\to T\\to W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach node is assigned a conditional distribution given its parents. The\njoint distribution then factorises as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(k,t,w)=p(k)p(t\\mid k)p(w\\mid t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor binary variables, five free probabilities suffice here instead of\nseven in an arbitrary joint table. In general, the factorisation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∏<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x_1,\\ldots,x_d)=\\prod_i p(x_i\\mid x_{\\mathrm{pa}(i)})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graph expresses model assumptions, not automatically a causal\nmechanism established by observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three elementary paths<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001539_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Kette, Gabel und Collider mit Status bei beobachtetem oder unbeobachtetem mittlerem Knoten.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Kette, Gabel und Collider mit Status bei\nbeobachtetem oder unbeobachtetem mittlerem Knoten.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Drei elementare Strukturen.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>D-separation determines whether the graph structure guarantees\nconditional independence. On a path with three nodes, observing the\nmiddle node blocks a chain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to B\\to C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor a fork\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\leftarrow B\\to C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a collider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to B\\leftarrow C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe opposite holds: without an observed collider or observed descendant\nof it, the path is blocked; observation can open it. On longer paths,\nall non-colliders must be unobserved and each collider itself or at\nleast one of its descendants must be observed for the path to be active.\nOnly if all paths are blocked does the graph guarantee the corresponding\nindependence. An active path does not necessarily imply actual\ndependence for every particular parametrisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Selection induces dependence<\/h2>\n<p>Two independent fault indicators\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach occur with probability 0.1. A detector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nraises an alarm when at least one fault occurs. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.9<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.19<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(C=1)=1-0.9^2=0.19<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nGiven an alarm,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A=1\\mid C=1)=10/19<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand similarly for B. The joint conditional probability is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/19<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(10/19)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen A is known for certain and an alarm is present, the conditional\nprobability of B returns to 0.1. The two faults thus compete to explain\nthe observation after selection on the alarm.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> fractions <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Fraction <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> F<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> alarm<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>alarm<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> joint<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>alarm<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (alarm,m,joint)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(F(<span class=\"dv\">19<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">19<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">19<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> joint<span class=\"op\">!=<\/span>m<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>m<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A common power supply S influences two sensors A and B, which are\nconditionally independent. Is marginal independence necessary? What\nchanges when S is observed?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: the fork path is open without S, so marginal independence\nis not guaranteed. Conditional on S, the only path is blocked, hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\perp B\\mid S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the model. In practice, further unaccounted-for paths can invalidate\nthis conclusion; residuals and domain assumptions should therefore be\nchecked. Instead of directed parent factors, undirected models use\nnon-negative potentials and a common normalising constant to represent\nsuch structural assumptions differently.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://inst.eecs.berkeley.edu/~cs188/textbook/bayes-nets/d-separation.html\">UC\nBerkeley CS188: D-Separation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · d-Separation<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~epxing/Class/10708-09/lecture.html\">Carnegie\nMellon 10-708: Probabilistic Graphical Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture overview\n8–10, sum-product and tree structure<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M18-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M18-L4 – Exact Inference and Message Passing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Master: Probabilistic Models and Bayesian Inference (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L3<\/a>,\nM4.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Perform marginalisation using\nfactors and sum-product messages.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From product to marginal distribution<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a binary state chain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to B\\to C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTo estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(A\\mid C=1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nall states of B can be enumerated. Variable elimination has the\nadvantage of storing intermediate results only over variables on which\nthey actually depend. For the query, evidence is first fixed; factors\ncontaining the variable to be eliminated are then multiplied and summed\nover that variable. Finally, the result is normalised. The size of\nintermediate factors depends on the elimination order; densely connected\nmodels may still require exponential storage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Messages on a chain<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001718_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Rückwärtsnachrichten von C über B an A und resultierender normierter Posterior.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Rückwärtsnachrichten von C über B an A\nund resultierender normierter Posterior.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Nachrichten für exakte Inferenz.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For the synthetic teaching example, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(A=1)=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(B=1\\mid A=0)=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(B=1\\mid A=1)=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(C=1\\mid B=0)=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(C=1\\mid B=1)=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe message from C to B contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_{C\\to B}=(0.1,0.9)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe next message is obtained by summation:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/munder><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_{B\\to A}(a)=\\sum_b p(b\\mid a)m_{C\\to B}(b).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe result is 0.26, and for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is 0.74. Multiplication by the prior\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.7,0.3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.182<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.222<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.182,0.222)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe evidence probability is 0.404, and the required posterior is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.222<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.404<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.5495<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.222/0.404\\approx0.5495<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic shows both messages and the subsequent normalisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Factor graph and general sum-product<\/h2>\n<p>A factor graph consists of variable nodes and factor nodes. A message\nfrom a variable to one of its factors is the product of the other\nincoming messages, multiplied by an evidence indicator if applicable.\nThe message from a factor to a variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis obtained by summing the factor multiplied by the incoming messages\nfrom all other neighbouring variables. If all values are non-negative\nand the evidence has positive mass, the marginal distribution of a\nvariable node can be obtained by normalising the product of its incoming\nmessages. On a tree, two suitable passes give exact marginals. On cyclic\ngraphs, iterative loopy belief propagation can provide an approximation\nwithout a general guarantee of convergence or exactness.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>prior<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.7<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> T<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>e<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> joint<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>prior[:,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>T<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>e<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>e<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>prior<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>m<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(m,[<span class=\"fl\">.26<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.74<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(z,<span class=\"fl\">.404<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose((prior<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>m)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>z,joint.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>joint.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>())<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A=1\\mid C=0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nSolution: the message from C is now\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.9,0.1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand that from B is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.74<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.26<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.74,0.26)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNormalisation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.078<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.518<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.078<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>39<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>298<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.1309<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.078/(0.518+0.078)=39/298\\approx0.1309<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn computationally intensive models, logarithmic representations or\nregular message normalisation help prevent numerical underflow. However,\nwhen normalising to compute the evidence probability, discarded scaling\nfactors must be accounted for.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://inst.eecs.berkeley.edu/~cs188/textbook/bayes-nets/elimination.html\">UC\nBerkeley CS188: Exact Inference in Bayes Nets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Variable\nelimination<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~epxing/Class/10708-09/lecture.html\">Carnegie\nMellon 10-708: Probabilistic Graphical Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture overview\n8–10, sum-product and tree structure<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M18-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M18-L5 – Latent Variables, Mixture Models and EM<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Master: Probabilistic Models and Bayesian Inference (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Estimate a Gaussian mixture\nmodel using EM and recognise local optima.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Unobserved group membership<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor provides voltage readings from two operating modes whose\nlabels are missing. A mixture models\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>K<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)=\\sum_{j=1}^K\\pi_j\\mathcal N(x;\\mu_j,\\sigma_j^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_j\\geq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_j\\pi_j=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTerm j describes the probability of the unobserved class and the\nmeasurement density within that class. The observed log-likelihood is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=\\sum_i\\log\\sum_j\\pi_j\\mathcal N(x_i;\\mu_j,\\sigma_j^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause of the sum inside the logarithm, it cannot be decomposed into\nindependent weighted mean problems without introducing additional latent\ninformation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Alternating between E and M steps<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001564_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zuständigkeiten der beiden Mischungskomponenten als Funktion der Spannung; Gleichheit bei 2 V.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zuständigkeiten der beiden\nMischungskomponenten als Funktion der Spannung; Gleichheit bei 2\nV.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Weiche Zuordnung in einer Gauß-Mischung.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In the E step, we compute the responsibility under the current\nparameters:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>;<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_{ij}=\\frac{\\pi_j\\mathcal N(x_i;\\mu_j,\\sigma_j^2)}{\\sum_h\\pi_h\\mathcal N(x_i;\\mu_h,\\sigma_h^2)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The M step maximises the expected complete-data log-likelihood. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_j=\\sum_i r_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_j&#39;=N_j/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_j&#39;=\\sum_i r_{ij}x_i/N_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>′<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_j^{2\\prime}=\\sum_i r_{ij}(x_i-\\mu_j&#39;)^2/N_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided the variance is freely estimated and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_j&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe simplify the exercise by fixing the variance at 1 V² so that only\nweights and means are updated. With equal weights and initial means of 0\nV and 4 V, the reading of 2 V has equal responsibility for both\ncomponents; for the reading of 1 V, the left component has\nresponsibility\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.982<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(1+e^{-4})\\approx0.982<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic illustrates these soft assignments.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical experiment<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.special <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> logsumexp<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> mu<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> pi<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> ll<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">25<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    logp<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.log(pi)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(x[:,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>mu)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.log(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    norm<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>logsumexp(logp,axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> ll.append(norm.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.exp(logp<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>norm[:,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> N<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>r.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    mu<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(r<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]).<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> pi<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(np.diff(ll)<span class=\"op\">&gt;=-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(pi,[<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(mu,ll[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],ll[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Exact EM does not decrease the observed likelihood: the E step makes\na lower bound exact at the current parameters and the M step improves\nthat bound. This is no guarantee of a global optimum. If variances are\nfreely estimated, a component can collapse around a single measurement\nand the likelihood can grow without bound. A minimum variance, suitable\npriors and multiple initialisations can help; component labels remain\ninterchangeable without an additional convention.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Three readings\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,2,4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave responsibilities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.9,0.5,0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor component 1. Calculate the new mean and weight. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_1=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_1&#39;=(0+1+0.4)/1.5=14/15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1&#39;=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe memberships are not confirmed operating labels; classification\nremains uncertain when components overlap strongly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://cs229.stanford.edu/notes_archive/cs229-notes7b.pdf\">Andrew\nNg, Stanford CS229: Mixtures of Gaussians and the EM algorithm<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · E/M\nsteps, mixture models; PDF opened directly, first page visually\nchecked<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M18-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M18-L6 – Markov Chain Monte Carlo and Diagnostics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Master: Probabilistic Models and Bayesian Inference (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Implement Metropolis–Hastings\nand interpret Monte Carlo diagnostics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sampling from a density known only up to\nproportionality<\/h2>\n<p>A posterior\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>π<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(x)=\\widetilde\\pi(x)/Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a normalising constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat is difficult to compute. Metropolis–Hastings generates a Markov\nchain whose transitions leave the target distribution invariant. From\nstate x a candidate y is proposed from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(y\\mid x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is accepted with probability<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mover><mi>π<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mover><mi>π<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha(x,y)=\\min\\left(1,\\frac{\\widetilde\\pi(y)q(x\\mid y)}{\\widetilde\\pi(x)q(y\\mid x)}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Otherwise, the chain remains at x. The unknown factor Z cancels. With\nsuitable proposals, the procedure satisfies detailed balance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(x)P(x,dy)=\\pi(y)P(y,dx)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the measure sense; this implies invariance. Convergence from\narbitrary initial states requires additional conditions (see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L9<\/a>).\nRejected proposals must not be removed from the stored trajectory (the\nunchanged state must be recorded).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Random-walk Metropolis<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001647_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Je 300 gespeicherte Zustände vier unabhängig gestarteter Metropolis-Ketten nach Aufwärmphase.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Je 300 gespeicherte Zustände vier\nunabhängig gestarteter Metropolis-Ketten nach Aufwärmphase.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Vier Metropolis-Ketten.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>As a model that can be checked, we choose the standard normal\ndistribution. With a symmetric normally distributed proposal of standard\ndeviation 1, the logarithm of the acceptance ratio simplifies to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-(y^2-x^2)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA proposal from 0 to 2 is thus accepted with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.1353<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2}\\approx0.1353<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe reverse move is accepted with certainty. The graphic shows excerpts\nfrom four independently started chains.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">42<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>chains<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> rates<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> start <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>start<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> out<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> acc<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">12000<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>rng.normal()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> np.log(rng.random())<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(y<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>y<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> acc<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> i<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2000<\/span>: out.append(x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    chains.append(out)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> rates.append(acc<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12000<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array(chains)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(a.mean(),a.std(),rates)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(a.mean())<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.12<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(a.std()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.12<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostics are not a certificate of convergence<\/h2>\n<p>The draws are correlated. For a stationary scalar quantity with\nintegrable autocorrelation, approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{eff}}=N/(1+2\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\rho_k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds, provided the sum is well defined. The Monte Carlo standard error\nof the mean is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/\\sqrt{N_{\\mathrm{eff}}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis differs from the posterior standard deviation s. Multiple chains\nwith dispersed starting values facilitate assessment. Rank-normalised\nsplit R-hat compares chains, while bulk and tail ESS assess different\nparts of the distribution. R-hat values near 1 and sufficient ESS are\nuseful but not sufficient evidence. Hamiltonian Monte Carlo uses\ngradients; there, additional features such as divergences must also be\nexamined. A histogram alone can be misleading when modes are poorly\nexplored.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>For 10,000 stationary draws, assume the idealised conditions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho_k=0.5^k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\rho_k=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10000<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathrm{eff}}=10000/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the standard error is approximately 0.0346. Twice as many correlated\ndraws should not be equated with twice as many independent draws. Before\napplying the algorithm to complex models, test it against known target\ndistributions and different starting points.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://mc-stan.org/rstan/reference/Rhat.html\">RStan:\nConvergence and efficiency diagnostics for Markov Chains<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nRank-normalised split R-hat, ESS<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/elias.koutsoupias/pc2017-18/lectures.html\">University\nof Oxford: Probability and Computing, Markov chains<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Stationarity,\nirreducibility, periodicity<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M18-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M18-L7 – Variational Inference and the ELBO<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Master: Probabilistic Models and Bayesian Inference (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L6<\/a>,\nM9.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the ELBO from KL\ndivergence and assess a mean-field approximation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Inference through optimisation<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe a latent variable, x be fixed data and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInstead of drawing correlated samples from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z\\mid x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nvariational inference seeks a tractable density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_\\lambda(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a parametrised family. For suitable densities and finite integrals,\nthe identity holds:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟<\/mo><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/munder><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p(x)=\\underbrace{E_q[\\log p(x,z)-\\log q(z)]}_{\\mathcal L(q)}+D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(q\\Vert p(z\\mid x)).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p(z\\mid x)=\\log p(x,z)-\\log p(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the definition of KL divergence. Since the divergence is\nnon-negative,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a lower bound on the log evidence (Evidence Lower Bound, ELBO).\nMaximising the ELBO for fixed data and a fixed model minimises the\nindicated direction of KL divergence. The identity does not mean the\nELBO is always easy to compute.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What does a factorised approximation lose?<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001641_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Konturen auf Mahalanobis-Radien 1, 2 und 3; wahrer korrelierter Posterior und diagonale Näherung mit Varianz 0,36.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Konturen auf Mahalanobis-Radien 1, 2 und\n3; wahrer korrelierter Posterior und diagonale Näherung mit Varianz\n0,36.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Mean Field verliert Korrelation.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Consider a two-dimensional normal posterior with mean zero, unit\nvariances and correlation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts precision matrix is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Lambda=(1-r^2)^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-r\\\\-r&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe restrict q to independent normal variables with mean vector m and\ndiagonal covariance D. For dimension d=2 the KL divergence is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tr<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(q\\Vert p)=\\tfrac12[\\operatorname{tr}(\\Lambda D)+m^\\top\\Lambda m-d+\\log(\\det\\Sigma/\\det D)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Differentiation with respect to the diagonal elements\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12(\\Lambda_{ii}-1/v_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe minimum is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_i=1/\\Lambda_{ii}=0.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe true marginal variances are each 1. The graphic compares density\ncontours on equal scales; the computationally convenient independence\nproduces marginals that are too narrow. The minimum KL value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.5108<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\tfrac12\\log(1-r^2)\\approx0.5108<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Optimisation methods and limitations<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.optimize <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> minimize<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>S<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>]])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> L<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.inv(S)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> kl(logv):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.exp(logv)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(np.diag(L)<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>v<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>np.linalg.slogdet(S)[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>logv.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>minimize(kl,np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> r.success <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(np.exp(r.x),<span class=\"fl\">.36<\/span>,atol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(r.fun)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Mean field decomposes q into factors; coordinate-wise updates can be\nanalytical in suitable exponential families. With stochastic gradients,\na normal variable can be represented, for example, as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>ϵ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=\\mu+\\sigma\\epsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith parameter-free normal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder suitable conditions, this allows gradients through the sample. For\nmultimodal targets, the direction of KL divergence may favour individual\nmodes. The ELBO curve alone does not measure the quality of every\nprediction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Set the correlation to 0.6. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.64<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_i=1-0.36=0.64<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nminimum KL value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.64<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.2231<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\log(0.64)/2\\approx0.2231<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen correlation is zero, the true posterior belongs to the mean-field\nfamily and KL is zero. In applications, predictive distributions,\nsensitivity and MCMC results on a smaller reference problem should be\ncompared.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.00670\">Blei, Kucukelbir,\nMcAuliffe (2017): Variational Inference: A Review for Statisticians<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· ELBO, KL, Mean Field<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M18-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M18-L8 – Gaussian Processes and Predictive Uncertainty<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Master: Probabilistic Models and Bayesian Inference (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M8, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate Gaussian process\nregression with a covariance kernel and interpret predictive\nvariance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A distribution over function values<\/h2>\n<p>For a calibration curve\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nuncertainty at unmeasured positions is important alongside the expected\ntrend. A Gaussian process\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒢<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\sim\\mathcal{GP}(m,k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndefines, for every finite set of inputs, a jointly normally distributed\nvector of function values with mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand covariance entries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(x_i,x_j)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA symmetric positive semidefinite kernel is required. The RBF kernel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>s<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(x,x&#39;)=s_f^2\\exp[-(x-x&#39;)^2/(2\\ell^2)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses amplitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand length scale\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSmall length scales allow more rapid local changes; the units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℓ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust match those of x.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conditioning on an observation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001580_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Posterior-Mittel und punktweise 95-Prozent-Intervalle der latenten Funktion nach einer Messung bei null.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Posterior-Mittel und punktweise\n95-Prozent-Intervalle der latenten Funktion nach einer Messung bei\nnull.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Gauß-Prozess nach einer Messung.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>With independent Gaussian measurement noise of variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_n^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a zero prior mean, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=k(X,X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_*=k(X,X_*)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi>*<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_{**}=k(X_*,X_*)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen, for the latent function at new points,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>K<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>I<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi>*<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>K<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>I<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_*=K_*^\\top(K+\\sigma_n^2 I)^{-1}y,\\qquad V_*=K_{**}-K_*^\\top(K+\\sigma_n^2 I)^{-1}K_*.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The formulas arise from the conditional multivariate normal\ndistribution. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_f=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_n^2=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a measurement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe mean at the measurement location is 1.6 and the latent variance is\n0.2. Far away, the covariance term tends to zero, the mean returns to 0\nand the variance to 1. When predicting a new independent measurement,\nthe noise variance is added. The graphic shows the latent pointwise\nuncertainty and the measurement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stable computation<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> Xs<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linspace(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">121<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> k(a,b): <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(a[:,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b[<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>,:])<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>L<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.cholesky(k(X,X)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.eye(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(X)))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>Ks<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>k(X,Xs)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.solve(L,Ks)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>mu<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>Ks.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>np.linalg.solve(L.T,np.linalg.solve(L,y))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>var<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(v<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>v).<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(var<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose([mu[<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>],var[<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>]],[<span class=\"fl\">1.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Cholesky decomposition avoids explicit matrix inversion. A dense\nexact GP typically requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomputation time and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmemory for factorisation. Inducing-point and structured methods reduce\ncosts under additional assumptions. Hyperparameters can be estimated\nthrough marginal likelihood; when fixed hyperparameter estimates are\nplugged in, the intervals do not reflect their uncertainty. Pointwise\n95% intervals are not simultaneous 95% bands over an entire curve.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>What latent variance and measurement prediction variance arise at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the above model? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(0,1)=e^{-1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlatent variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.25<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.7057<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-e^{-1}/1.25\\approx0.7057<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmeasurement prediction variance approximately 0.9557. A narrower\ninterval is not automatically better; calibration against held-out\nmeasurements and checking the assumed noise structure are necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.tufts.edu/cs/145/2026s/notes/day02-GaussianProcesses.html\">Tufts\nUniversity CS145: Gaussian Processes for Regression<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Kernel,\nposterior prediction<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M18-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M18-L9 – Stochastic Processes, Markov Chains and Ergodicity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Master: Probabilistic Models and Bayesian Inference (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Optional advanced study<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model finite Markov chains and\ndistinguish invariance, ergodicity and convergence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>States over time<\/h2>\n<p>A technical system switches daily between normal operation N and\nmaintenance W (the original state identifiers are retained). A\nstochastic process is a family of random variables\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor a time-homogeneous discrete Markov chain,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X_{t+1}=j\\mid X_0,\\ldots,X_t=i)=P_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds. The state must contain enough information for this assumption to\nbe plausible. For maintenance duration with memory, the time since the\nstart of maintenance, for example, would be a useful additional state\nvariable. A transition matrix is row-stochastic: all entries are\nnon-negative and every row sums to 1.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stationary distribution and convergence<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001635_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Wartungswahrscheinlichkeit nähert sich bei beiden Startzuständen dem stationären Wert 0,20.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Wartungswahrscheinlichkeit nähert sich\nbei beiden Startzuständen dem stationären Wert 0,20.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Konvergenz einer Markovkette.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The synthetic matrix for N and W is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=\\begin{pmatrix}0.9&amp;0.1\\\\0.4&amp;0.6\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For row distributions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{t+1}=p_tP<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_t=p_0P^t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhold. A stationary distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi P=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_N+\\pi_W=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1\\pi_N=0.4\\pi_W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.8,0.2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStarting from normal operation, the probability of maintenance after one\nday is 0.1 and after two days\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.9\\cdot0.1+0.1\\cdot0.6=0.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe recurrence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{t+1}=0.1+0.5w_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_t=0.2+(w_0-0.2)0.5^t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic shows the trajectory from both initial states.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Long-term statements and a counterexample<\/h2>\n<p>A finite irreducible chain has a unique stationary distribution.\nIrreducible means that every state can be reached from every other state\nin finitely many steps with positive probability. With additional\naperiodicity, the distribution converges to this stationary distribution\nfrom any initial distribution. The period of a state is the greatest\ncommon divisor of its possible positive return times. The matrix with\nzero on the diagonal and one off the diagonal is irreducible but\nperiodic; its stationary distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1/2,1/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unique, yet from a fixed state the marginal distribution oscillates.\nTime averages can nevertheless converge. For finite irreducible chains,\nan ergodic theorem holds for time averages of integrable state\nfunctions; the term ergodic is used more narrowly or broadly depending\non the source.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>P<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>]])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> pi<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(pi<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>P,pi)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> n <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>np.linalg.matrix_power(P,n)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(p[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>n))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(np.linalg.matrix_power(P,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A system starts in W. What is the probability of W on day three?\nSolution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.5<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_3=0.2+0.8\\cdot0.5^3=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf maintenance were permanently absorbing, however, the transition from\nW to N would be zero and the original stationary distribution would no\nlonger be valid. For MCMC (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L6<\/a>),\na constructed transition rule must not only leave the target invariant\nbut also explore the relevant state space sufficiently. Continuous and\nnon-homogeneous processes require further theory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/elias.koutsoupias/pc2017-18/lectures.html\">University\nof Oxford: Probability and Computing, Markov chains<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Stationarity,\nirreducibility, periodicity<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
